---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About">
  <section class="py-8 space-y-8 max-w-2xl">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">About Me</h1>
    
    <div class="space-y-4 text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        I'm Brodie, an Industrial-Organizational Psychology master's graduate with a deep 
        curiosity for how systems (human and digital) actually work.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm a Systems Analyst on the Data Governance team in Purdue University's 
        Institutional Data Analytics + Assessment office. I support Elements, the faculty 
        reporting system Purdue uses to track research, teaching, and service: keeping its 
        data accurate, configuring it to fit campus needs, and helping faculty 
        get the most out of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that, I worked in the <a href="https://earchives.lib.purdue.edu/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Digital Programs unit</a> of Purdue University Libraries and School 
        of Information Studies, turning history into accessible digital records for the world 
        to see. It was meticulous, detail-oriented work at the intersection of preservation 
        and technology, and it's where I started building scripts and tools to automate 
        digitization and OCR workflows.
      </p>
      <p>
        By night (and most other hours), I'm exploring AI as a power tool — not just for 
        productivity, but as a way to think about delegation, autonomy, and systems design. 
        Most of that happens in Claude Code for AI-assisted development, along with 
        documentation templating, data wrangling, and automating workflows that are ripe for 
        optimization. I've also worked with vision-language models through Ollama and with 
        agent orchestration. Accessibility and data accuracy come first in everything I build, 
        so independent human verification of AI output is essential to how I work.
      </p>
      <p>
        My thesis examined how staff perceive organizational performance management systems 
        and explored alternatives that emphasize the importance of staff accepting the tool 
        that measures their own performance. As an underrepresented population in the empirical 
        literature, institutional staff deserve to have their perspectives help inform how they 
        are assessed.
      </p>
      <p>
        I believe the best tools disappear into the workflow, and the best systems make 
        complexity feel simple.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
